TerrainRenderer is deeply tied to `VertexMap` (Core map storage) — its Render methods take VertexMap directly and use VertexMapChunkMeshBuilder (Core). So TerrainRenderer can't move to Render as-is. Per my earlier design: the renderer should consume `VertexMapChunkMeshData` (already in Abstractions); the Core-side glue builds meshes from VertexMap via the builder and feeds them.

That's a REAL refactor of RaylibTerrainRenderer's Render path: today it does chunk visibility computation + builder invocation internally. To decouple: move the chunk-visibility + build orchestration to... hmm, wait. Let me reconsider: who calls TerrainRenderer.Render(VertexMap,...)? RaylibHostLoop presumably. The visibility computation uses camera + HexCoordinates + VertexChunk.ChunkSize — pure constants! HexCoordinates is a Core type (stayed). 

Option: renderer API becomes `Render(VertexMapChunkMeshData meshData...)`? But per-chunk building driven by visibility — the renderer decides WHICH chunks to build based on camera. That logic needs map access.

Cleaner decoupling: define an interface in Abstractions:
```csharp
public interface IVertexMapChunkMeshSource {
    int WidthInChunks { get; } int HeightInChunks { get; }
    void BuildChunk(int chunkX, int chunkY, VertexMapChunkMeshData dst); // wraps offsets etc.
}
```
Core-side adapter (in Client.Raylib or Adapter) implements it over VertexMap+builder. TerrainRenderer consumes the interface. HexCoordinates constants — needed by renderer for spacing; HexCoordinates stayed in Core! The renderer uses HexWidth/RowSpacing/ChunkSize constants. Options: pass as params (chunkSizeCm, hexWidth, rowSpacing) or move HexCoordinates to Abstractions... HexCoordinates is "常量 HexWidth/RowSpacing/GetChunkKey" readonly struct — pure constants, 38 refs. Moving it to Abstractions would be consistent with WorldCmInt2 etc. But it's map-domain... it's needed by the render mesh math. Decision: move HexCoordinates.cs to Abstractions too (it's a pure constant struct, zero deps — same class of thing as WorldCmInt2). GetChunkKey deps? Check file. If it has only constants + pure statics, move it.

Then VertexChunk.ChunkSize — used by renderer. VertexChunk is heavy SoA data class (stays Core). The renderer uses only the constant ChunkSize/ChunkSizeMask. Extract those constants? Pass as param to the source interface instead: the interface's BuildChunk hides chunk math; the renderer still needs chunk world spacing for visibility: pass `chunkWorldWidth/Height` via interface properties. Let me design:

```csharp
public interface ITerrainChunkMeshSource
{
    int WidthInChunks { get; }
    int HeightInChunks { get; }
    float ChunkWorldWidthMeters { get; }
    float ChunkWorldHeightMeters { get; }
    bool TryBuildChunk(int chunkX, int chunkY, VertexMapChunkMeshData dst);
}
```
Renderer visibility loop uses those. Core adapter implements over VertexMap + HexCoordinates + builder. Water plane drawing (VisualHeightmap path) — separate method already.

This keeps the renderer's actual draw code unchanged. The visibility/build orchestration splits: loop stays in renderer (it knows camera), source provides data. 

OK — but this is getting deep. TerrainRenderer refactor: I'll implement it now as part of C2a/b. Let me read the whole TerrainRenderer file to do this correctly. 250-ish lines? Let me read it.

