Create `GalleryFont.cs`:
```csharp
public static class GalleryFont
{
    public static Font Ui;  // loaded once
    public static void Load() {
        // collect codepoints: ASCII 32..126 + every char in SceneCatalog titles/summaries + fixed UI strings
        // candidate font paths: msyh.ttc / simhei.ttf (Windows), /usr/share/fonts noto CJK (Linux)
        // LoadFontEx(path, baseSize=64, codepoints, count) — baseSize 64 to allow crisp 18/20/28 rendering
        // fail-loud if none loadable
    }
}
```
And a helper `GalleryFont.Draw(string text, int x, int y, float size, Color c)` wrapping DrawTextEx with spacing 1f, plus Measure.

Then replace all `Rl.DrawText(` in gallery with `GalleryFont.Draw(` (scenes' HUD text is ASCII mostly, but uniformity + MaterialBinding labels are Chinese? `_slots[i].Label` — probably Chinese labels like "不透明/半透明". Scene titles Chinese. So codepoints from catalog + all drawn strings dynamically — simplest: build codepoints from ALL scene Title+Summary + static UI strings + ASCII range + common CJK used in labels... MaterialBinding labels are in scene file constants — collect from... can't easily enumerate every $-interpolated string. Robust approach: load the FULL CJK basic range 0x4E00-0x9FCC (~21k glyphs) + ASCII. Concern: atlas size/time with TTC. raylib LoadFontEx with 21k codepoints at baseSize 32: glyph atlas probably 2-4 textures 2048²... could take a second or two and ~64MB RAM — acceptable for gallery startup? Safer + faster: dynamic subset — collect the union of chars from: SceneCatalog descriptors (titles/summaries), plus a static "KnownUiText" list that scenes register... complex.

Middle ground: codepoints = ASCII + the chars extracted from all scene Title/Summary + menu strings + MaterialBinding labels etc. Get those by scanning the scene assemblies' string literals? Overkill.

Practical: extract chars at runtime from SceneCatalog descriptors + a few known UI literals, AND include CJK punctuation range + a buffer: full range 0x4E00-0x9FA5 (20902) is simplest and bulletproof — let me just try full range with simhei.ttf (TTF faster than TTC?). Test load time; if slow (>2s), narrow. Actually 20902 glyphs at 32px in raylib → atlas 8×2048²? raylib packs into multiple 2048 textures — memory ~20902×32×32×4 ≈ 85MB — heavy but gallery-only, one-time. Hmm, better use baseSize 20 (we draw at 18/20/28 — 28 upscaled from 20 = slightly blurry title). Compromise baseSize 28: ~65MB. Meh.

Alternative smarter: measure-driven subset — collect codepoints from SceneCatalog titles/summaries + Program's literals + MaterialBinding labels exposed as... I can add to SceneDescriptor? No — simplest reliable: gallery strings are finite; collect ALL char literals used by scanning the compiled strings: iterate SceneCatalog descriptors AND scan each scene instance's drawn text? Not accessible pre-draw.

Decision: full CJK range 0x4E00-0x9FFF + ASCII + CJK punctuation (0x3000-0x303F) + full-width (0xFF00-0xFFEF), baseSize 24, font simhei.ttf (single TTF, no TTC index issues — raylib LoadFontEx on TTC may need font index handling; TTF safer). If load fails try msyh. Draw sizes 18/20/28 — 28 title from 24 base = minor upscale, acceptable; or baseSize 28. Let's do 28.

Wait — actually check raylib-cs vendored binding has LoadFontEx with int[] codepoints overload? Check binding signature.

