FOUND IT — the binding's Mesh has **raylib 5.5 layout including `boneMatrices` + `boneCount`** (5.5 added these AFTER boneWeights, before vaoId/vboId). If the NATIVE raylib.dll is actually 5.0/5.1 (without boneMatrices/boneCount in Mesh), then binding struct is BIGGER than native → by-value marshal of the struct to DrawMesh writes/pads beyond native size, and native interprets vaoId/vboId at wrong offsets → garbage VAO → AV for meshes with populated data (models), while nulls → 0 → default upload path works (ground cube).

Hmm wait — if layout mismatched, DrawModelEx would ALSO crash (it marshals Model by value containing meshes pointer — pointer only, no struct copy! DrawModelEx(Model model, ...) by value copies the Model STRUCT (fixed size, contains pointers) — meshes stay a POINTER, native dereferences with NATIVE layout — consistent ✓). Only MY `model.meshes[i]` path marshals a Mesh STRUCT COPY through the boundary using the BINDING layout → mismatch → AV. That's why DrawModelEx works but DrawMesh(mesh copy) crashes!

Verify: check native raylib.dll version string. `strings raylib.dll | grep -i "raylib 5"`? Or check how other code draws model meshes — grep DrawMesh usage with model meshes... Renderer code uses DrawMesh only for OWN meshes (GenMesh/procedural) — never model.meshes[i]! So the binding Mesh layout vs native was never exercised with pointer-full meshes via DrawMesh.

Fix WITHOUT touching vendored binding: don't pass Mesh by value from model — instead **draw model meshes via DrawModelEx in the shadow pass** (works! uses native-consistent path): DrawModelEx(model, pos, rotAxis, rotAngle, scale, WHITE) with the DEPTH material?? DrawModelEx uses model's own materials... raylib has DrawModelEx only with model.materials. To draw model with a REPLACEMENT material: temporarily set `model.materials[i].shader = depthShader` (and remember/restore), call DrawModelEx with dummy... DrawModelEx signature (model, position, rotationAxis, rotationAngle, scale, tint) — internally per mesh: DrawMesh(mesh, materials[meshMaterial[i]], transform). So: swap model materials' shader to depth shader → DrawModelEx → restore. That reuses the native path ✓ no layout issue.

Update RaylibDirectionalShadowMap.DrawModelShadow:
```csharp
public void DrawModelShadow(Model model, Vector3 position, float rotationAngleY, Vector3 scale)
{
    // 换装深度着色器（经 DrawModelEx 原生路径绘制，避免 Mesh 按值封送）
    Shader* or copy originals? Model is class-passed by value struct copy — modifying model.materials[i].shader mutates the REAL model (pointer) — save & restore originals.
    for i: original[i] = model.materials[i].shader; model.materials[i].shader = _depthShader;
    Rl.DrawModelEx(model, position, Vector3.UnitY, rotationAngleY, scale, Color.WHITE);
    restore;
}
```
Also DrawMeshShadow(mesh,...) for GenMesh cubes: same by-value Mesh marshal — ground worked because nulls... but AFTER first upload vaoId non-null — subsequent frames still worked (5 frames ran OK with 2 ground draws — vaoId populated after frame 1, frames 2-5 fine!) — so by-value Mesh marshal apparently didn't corrupt for ground even with vaoId set... hmm — that weakens my layout theory? If layout mismatched by 2 fields, vaoId read from wrong offset (garbage from padding/next struct bytes) would crash frame 2... unless marshalled copies zero-init padding and native reads vaoId=0 → re-upload each frame (works, slow but no crash). Plausible.

For safety, make DrawMeshShadow ALSO go through a safe path? GenMesh cube path worked across frames — keep DrawMeshShadow (ground/props fine) but note. Actually to be thorough: raylib native Mesh in 5.5 DOES include boneMatrices+boneCount; if native dll is truly 5.5, layouts match and my theory dies... test native version: strings on dll.

